[Event "Slav: Main line 4...dxc4"]

{The Slav (2...c6) holds d5 while keeping the c8-bishop's diagonal open.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
2. c4 {Queen's Gambit. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and eyes the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... c6 {[%csl Gd5] The Slav. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and supports the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
3. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
3... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
4. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
4... dxc4 {Takes the pawn on c4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. a4 {Stops ...b5. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on a4 takes b5 away from the opponent.}
5... Bf5 {Develops outside the chain. The bishop on f5 controls e4.}
6. e3 {Prepares Bxc4. Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
 (6. Ne5 {Hits c4 with the knight. The knight goes to e5. It attacks the pawn on c4, which is undefended.}
6... Nbd7 {Challenges e5. Develops the knight to d7, where it eyes the knight on e5. White's last move put a knight on e5, which this now pressures.}
7. Nxc4 {Regains the pawn. Takes the pawn on c4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
7... Qc7 {Prepares ...e5. The queen goes to c7 and controls e5.}
8. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
8... e5 {Strikes the centre. The pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
9. dxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5; Black will want to recapture.}
9... Nxe5 {Recaptures on e5, restoring the material balance.}
10. Bf4 {Pins. Develops the bishop to f4, where it eyes the knight on e5. Black's last move put a knight on e5, which this now pressures.}
10... Nfd7 {Supports e5. Regroups the knight to d7 and supports the knight on e5.}
11. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3, e4 and d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
)
6... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and supports the bishop on f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
7. Bxc4 {Regains. Takes the pawn on c4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
7... Bb4 {Pins. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8... Nbd7 {Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.} *

[Event "Slav: 4.e3 & Exchange"]

{Quieter tries.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
2. c4 {Queen's Gambit. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and eyes the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... c6 {Slav. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and supports the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
3. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
 (3. cxd5 {The Exchange. Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
3... cxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
4. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5. Black's last move put a pawn on d5, which this now pressures.}
4... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
5. Bf4 {Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
5... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
6. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
6... Bf5 {Develops the bishop to f5, where it controls e4.}
)
3... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
4... Bf5 {Develops the bishop to f5, where it controls e4.}
 (4... a6 {The Chebanenko: prepares ...b5. The pawn on a6 controls b5.}
5. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
5... b5 {[%cal Gb5c4] Gains queenside space. The pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent.}
)
5. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
5... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 supports the pawn on d5 and the bishop on f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
6. Nh4 {Hunts the bishop. The knight goes to h4.}
6... Bg6 {Regroups the bishop to g6 and controls e4.}
7. Nxg6 {Takes the bishop on g6; Black will want to recapture.}
7... hxg6 {Recaptures, opening the h-file. Recaptures on g6, restoring the material balance.} *
